TASK 1 — DISCOVERY (live code and live schema, not the prompt's description)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Script: apps/api/scripts/discover_fee32.py

Part 1 SQL confirmed deployed, nothing re-created:

  portfolio.positions.account_id  uuid NULL
    FK positions_account_id_fkey → public.accounts(id)
    partial index positions_account_idx (account_id)
      WHERE account_id IS NOT NULL AND system_to IS NULL

  public.portfolio_precedence_household_overrides
    (id, org_id, household_id, source_order jsonb, reason, approved_by,
     created_at, valid_from, valid_to, system_from, system_to)
    FKs → households(id), organizations(id)
    partial unique (org_id, household_id) WHERE system_to IS NULL
    RLS enabled, standard NULLIF + super-admin policy
    app_service: SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE

SIX CONFLICTS WITH THE PROMPT. Live won in every case.

 1. resolve_precedence's real signature is
      resolve_precedence(conn, org_id, position_candidates, *, apply=True)
    `apply` is keyword-only; the third argument is an iterable of ids OR
    records, not "ids".

 2. THE IMPORT PATH DOES NOT CALL resolve_precedence.
    portfolio_import.py:596 is `if resolve_precedence_after and touched:` and
    calls resolve_holding(), which gathers the candidates itself and then
    delegates. routers/portfolio_ingest.py:123 does the same. The prompt named
    the wrong callee; the line number was right. Consequence for the design:
    the household cannot come from a caller argument, it has to be derived from
    the rows resolve_precedence fetches for itself.

 3. portfolio_precedence_household_overrides LIVES IN `public`, NOT in the
    `portfolio` schema, despite the `portfolio_` name prefix. Qualifying it as
    `portfolio.` would fail at runtime. Recorded in the code as a comment on
    TABLE_HOUSEHOLD_OVERRIDES because the name actively invites the wrong guess.

 4. create_position() (services/portfolio_assets.py:542) is the SINGLE
    chokepoint for every position write — importer, API and manual edit all go
    through it — and it did not accept or write account_id at all. Part 1's
    column was unreachable from application code.

 5. [FIND] A LIVE BUG CREATED BY PART 1, not mentioned in the prompt.
    update_position() restates a position by closing the current row and
    re-inserting a successor through create_position() from an EXPLICIT column
    list. That list had no account_id. So the FIRST edit of any account-linked
    position would silently NULL its account link — the holding would keep its
    numbers and quietly detach from the statement it came from, with nothing
    raising. Fixed under Task 2; check 3 reproduces the drop and proves the fix.

 6. positions_account_id_fkey REFERENCES accounts(id) WITH NO ORG PREDICATE.
    Another tenant's account id satisfies the FK. That is a hard refusal, not a
    reviewable warning — see Task 2.

Other measured facts: accounts.household_id nullable; entities.primary_household_id
exists and is nullable; account_owners is bi-temporal with no unique constraint;
households has no temporal columns; app_service rolbypassrls=False (postgres
bypasses); every table this sprint touches was empty on dev; and NO org has a
portfolio.precedence.* org_settings row — every org is on DEFAULT_SETTINGS.


TASK 2 — POSITION ↔ ACCOUNT LINKAGE, VALIDATED IN APPLICATION CODE
-------------------------------------------------------------------
New: apps/api/services/portfolio_account_link.py
     apps/api/migrations/fee32_position_account_exceptions.sql

THREE outcomes, deliberately not two:

  1. Account is not this org's        → REFUSED (AccountLinkError), before the
                                        row exists. The FK is org-blind, so this
                                        check is the only tenant boundary. If it
                                        were a warning, the exception list would
                                        be the audit trail of a cross-tenant
                                        reference that had already succeeded.
  2. Account is ours, owner is not
     one of its active owners         → WRITTEN + reviewable exception recorded.
  3. Owner is an active owner         → nothing but the position.

No DB trigger, no CHECK constraint — per the RFC, and matching how
ownership_basis is already validated on this table.

WHY A NEW EXCEPTION TABLE RATHER THAN fee31's account_import_exceptions.
Reusing it was the first choice and does not fit, structurally:
  · batch_id is NOT NULL and FKs to account_import_batches. Two of the three
    position write paths have no custody batch to point at, and inventing one
    would put a fake row in the batch list an operator would try to re-drive.
  · record_kind CHECK admits only ('account','balance','flow').
  · source_row is NOT NULL and means "line N of the uploaded file". A manual
    single-position edit has no line number, and 0 would be a lie, not an
    absence.
Making it fit meant dropping a NOT NULL, widening a CHECK, and keeping a column
meaningless for two of three paths. public.position_account_exceptions carries
the SAME review shape (reason_code + human reason + jsonb detail + an
open/reviewed axis) so a future combined screen can union the two.

Two reason codes, kept distinct on purpose:
  owner_not_account_owner  — owners exist, none is this entity → mis-mapped import
  account_has_no_owners    — no active owners at all → incomplete account record
Different findings, different fixes, different people. Folding them into one
code hides the second inside the first.

Idempotency: partial unique on (org_id, position_id, account_id, reason_code)
WHERE reviewed_at IS NULL. Re-validating the same position does not duplicate;
a mismatch RE-RAISED after a reviewer closed the previous one is new information
and is recorded again. An index over all rows would swallow that silently.

The detail blob records the account's active owners AT THE TIME OF THE WRITE —
"who does own this account" is the first question a reviewer asks, and
re-deriving it later reads ownership as it is then, not as it was. Only
account_number_masked is ever stored; public.accounts holds no unmasked number
and this column does not become where one reappears.

Write paths wired: create_position (the chokepoint), update_position (carries
account_id forward — bug 5 above), POST/PATCH /portfolio/positions, and
import_positions_file(account_id=...), which pre-flights the cross-tenant check
ONCE before parsing rather than turning one tenant refusal into 400 identical
row errors and an import that reports partial success.

Reads: account_id / account_number_masked / account_household_id added to the
positions grid and detail payloads; GET /portfolio/position-account-exceptions
and POST .../{id}/review for the review list.


TASK 3 — HOUSEHOLD-LEVEL PRECEDENCE OVERRIDE
---------------------------------------------
Extended (not wrapped, not replaced) services/portfolio_precedence.py.

Resolution order is now:
  1. active row in portfolio_precedence_household_overrides for the household
  2. org_settings['portfolio.precedence.source_order']   ← unchanged
  3. DEFAULT_SETTINGS fallback                            ← unchanged

The only structural change to the existing function: get_source_order() used to
be called BEFORE the candidate fetch. It now runs after, as
_resolve_source_order(), because the order is no longer a property of the org
alone — it can be a property of the household these specific candidates belong
to, and the household is derived from the rows. Everything after that line is
byte-identical.

Household derivation, per the RFC: account_id → accounts.household_id when the
position carries an account; else owner_entity_id → entities.primary_household_id.
An account whose household_id is NULL does NOT fall back to the entity route —
the account is present and says "no household", which is an answer, not an
absence.

AMBIGUITY IS REFUSED, NOT GUESSED. Candidates share a holding key but need not
share an account_id (the normal shape is one custodial row with an account and
one manual row without). Each candidate maps to a household independently:
exactly one distinct → that override applies; none → org level; MORE THAN ONE →
no override, org level, and the reason is reported on the outcome. Picking "the
first one" would make which family's policy governs a holding depend on row
insertion order, and the winner would flip on re-resolution with no setting
having changed. Proved by check A.

An unusable stored override falls through to the org level, exactly as an
invalid org setting falls through to the default, and for the same reason:
resolution is on the ingestion path and failing an import because a settings row
went stale turns a configuration problem into a data problem.

superseded_by_source behaviour is untouched — same non-destructive single-column
UPDATE, same clear-the-winner half.

Management: set_household_source_order / clear_household_source_order, bi-temporal
on the SYSTEM axis to match the deployed partial unique index. Nothing FKs to
this table's id, so the valid axis would have worked; the system axis is chosen
because a changed override is a NEW POLICY DECISION with its own approver and
reason, not a correction of what the previous decision was — the superseded row
keeps its own reason and approved_by, which is the audit value of the table.
reason and approved_by are required, never defaulted. approved_by is the
caller's own verified user id and is NOT accepted from the request body: an
approver a request could name is not an approver.

Endpoints: GET/PUT/DELETE /portfolio/precedence/households/{household_id}.
POST /portfolio/precedence/resolve now also reports order_origin, household_id
and household_reason — "Addepar won" and "Addepar won because this household
overrides the firm's order" are different answers and only the second tells an
operator where to go to change it.


VERIFICATION — 11/11 PASS
--------------------------
apps/api/scripts/verify_fee32.py. Two disposable orgs, created and torn down per
run; nothing writes to a real org.

 [1]  PASS  account_id exists, uuid, NULLABLE, FK → accounts(id); a directly-held
            position (entity_direct, no account) inserts with account_id=NULL and
            resolves precedence cleanly. The owner's primary_household_id DOES
            point at the override household, so this also exercises the entity
            route with no account present.
 [2]  PASS  Mismatched account_id: position WRITTEN (open on both temporal axes,
            account_id persisted) + exactly one owner_not_account_owner exception
            recording the real owners. The ownerless account raises
            account_has_no_owners instead, so the two findings stay distinct.
            Comes back from the API's own read path, not just the table.
            Re-validation is idempotent.
 [2b] PASS  EXTRA — a cross-tenant account_id is REFUSED (AccountLinkError) and
            org A's position count is unchanged. Not in the sprint's list; it is
            the one case where "written and flagged" would be wrong.
 [3]  PASS  A genuine owner produces no exception — AND the restated successor of
            an edit still carries account_id (reproduces bug 5, proves the fix).
 [4]  PASS  Household override beats the org order on the SAME three rows and the
            same holding key. Proved in both directions: org order
            [addepar, altruist, manual] → addepar; household override
            [altruist, addepar, manual] → altruist, origin=household_override.
            The check also asserts the two orders pick DIFFERENT winners, so the
            fixture cannot pass vacuously.
 [5]  PASS  A no-override household resolves identically to the PRE-SPRINT code —
            compared against the real previous revision of
            services/portfolio_precedence.py, extracted from git and imported as
            a separate module, run over the SAME rows with apply=False on both
            sides. The revision is located with `git log -S <marker>` on a string
            this sprint introduced, then stepped back one commit, so the check
            keeps working AFTER this sprint commits (a HEAD-based comparison
            would compare the sprint to itself — the contamination recorded on a
            previous sprint). The check ALSO runs the same comparison on the
            OVERRIDE household and requires it to DIVERGE; without that, the
            match above would pass just as well against a household layer that
            never fires.
 [6]  PASS  superseded_by_source across FOUR states, not one:
              override v1 (altruist first) → altruist  (addepar=altruist, altruist=NULL, manual=altruist)
              override v2 (manual first)   → manual    (addepar=manual, altruist=manual, manual=NULL)
              override removed             → addepar   (addepar=NULL, altruist=addepar, manual=addepar)
            Each transition asserts the winner is CLEARED and every loser marked.
            Read back from the column, not from the returned outcome. The v1 row
            is also asserted to survive the change with its own reason intact.
 [7]  PASS  Cross-org isolation under app_service (rolbypassrls=False asserted
            FIRST, on its own — otherwise every denial below is vacuous). Org B
            has a REAL planted override row. Under org A's GUC: 0 of org B's
            visible, 2 of org A's OWN visible (so the read is isolated, not
            merely broken), unqualified SELECT returns exactly those 2, and a
            cross-org INSERT is refused by WITH CHECK.
            position_account_exceptions isolates identically.
 [R]  PASS  EXTRA — an exception can be reviewed; close is idempotent; the closed
            row leaves the OPEN list but stays in the full list; the same
            mismatch re-raised afterwards opens a SECOND row (the partial index
            is on open rows only, deliberately).
 [A]  PASS  EXTRA — candidates spanning two households apply NO override, fall
            through to the org level, and report why.
 [8]  PASS  Teardown restored the exact pre-test count on all 11 touched tables.
            By fixture org id, never a TRUNCATE.

Regression: verify_portfoliob 50/50 + 2 BLOCKED (Altruist credentials) and
verify_portfolioux1 72/72 — both their recorded baselines, unchanged. UX1 covers
the positions grid, which this sprint added three columns to (account_id,
account_number_masked, account_household_id) and one editable field to.

verify_portfolioux4 reports 70/71, and the one failure is NOT a fee32
regression — it is that script reading its own output. Measured, not assumed:

  check_task1c() does `git show HEAD:<router>` and asserts the routers had NO
  permissions envelope and published the editable lists unconditionally. That
  was the PRE-SPRINT state. Evaluating the check's exact predicate at two refs:

    HEAD                     no_envelope=False unconditional=False → FAIL
    1c64199 (ux4's commit)^  no_envelope=True  unconditional=True  → PASS

  1c64199 is verify_portfolioux4's own sprint commit — the one that ADDED the
  envelope. The moment it landed, HEAD stopped being the pre-sprint state and
  the check began asserting the absence of a fix that is now present.

  And the decisive control: fee32's working tree changes ZERO lines matching
  permissions / EDITABLE_FIELDS / CORRECTABLE_FIELDS / vocabularies in either of
  those two routers. (fee32 adds `account_id` to PositionCreate/PositionPatch
  and three read-only columns; it does not touch the envelope.)

  This is the SAME failure shape already recorded on this project ("a pre-sprint
  check that reads HEAD is valid only until the sprint commits; locate the ref
  with `git log -S <marker>` + `^`"). NOT fixed here — verify_portfolioux4.py
  belongs to another sprint that is itself still HELD, and quietly editing its
  evidence during someone else's review is worse than reporting it. Logged as a
  follow-up instead. verify_fee32's own check 5 uses the `git log -S` + `^`
  anchor precisely so it does not acquire this bug.

[FIND] Running two verify scripts CONCURRENTLY makes both unreliable. A first
attempt ran ux1 and ux4 at the same time and ux1 reported 71/72 with a teardown
drift of exactly ux4's fixtures ({transactions: 0→2, positions: 0→2, assets:
0→2, entities: 24→26, users: 21→25}). These scripts assert on GLOBAL table
counts, not on their own fixture tags, so a second script's rows read as the
first script's leak. Re-run sequentially: 72/72. Worth knowing before anyone
tries to parallelise the suite.


OUT OF SCOPE, CONFIRMED
------------------------
Joint-custody positions split into multiple owner rows on import — known,
confirmed gap per the Portfolio Reporting Layer thread. Not addressed here.

No backfill of account_id was performed and none is intended. Directly-held
assets and SPV interests (portfolio.assets.internal_spv_id) stay NULL by design.

No frontend was built for the two new read surfaces (the linkage exception
review list, and the household override editor). Both are served by real,
permission-gated endpoints; neither has a screen yet.
